    <name>
      <text>Determining  Arc Length-Q3 f(x)</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Determine the length of  the curve whose equation is given by \( \displaystyle y= {@functx@}\),  for \({@lower_x@}\leq x\leq{@upper_x@}\). Give your answer correct to two decimal places.</p>
<p>\(L=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]][[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>worked solution</p>
<p>Let's take the derivative of the given function f(x) with respect to x, and then plug it into the ds formula.</p>
<p>\(\frac{dy}{dx}={@d_functx@}\)</p>
<p>So that
\(
\begin{align}
ds &= \sqrt{1+[\frac{dy}{dx}]^{2}}dy \\
  &= {@ds@}\;dy \\
\end{align}
\)
</p>
<p>Now, the integral giving the arc length is,</p>
<p>\[L= \int ds=\int_{{@lower_x@}}^{{@upper_x@}}{@ds@}\;dx\]</p>
<p>To evaluate this integral, use any  appropriate method learned earlier. This should give.</p>
<p>\[\displaystyle \int  {@ds@}\;dx={@lfx@}.\]</p>
<p>Now the arclength is \[L=\int_{{@lower_x@}}^{{@upper_x@}}{@ds@}\;dx={@ta1@}\] </p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
